editorial:
## The four questions

- **State:** `dp[i]` = the number of ways to reach step `i`.
- **Transition:** the last move was either 1 step (from `i-1`) or 2 steps (from `i-2`), and those sets of sequences are disjoint — so `dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2]`.
- **Base:** `dp[0] = 1` (one way to stand at the bottom: do nothing), `dp[1] = 1`.
- **Order:** increasing `i`.

That recurrence is Fibonacci. Specifically `dp[n] = fib(n+1)`.

## Why the transitions are disjoint

This is the step worth checking rather than assuming. Every sequence reaching step `i` has a last move, and it is either 1 or 2 — never both. So no sequence is counted twice, and the sum is exact. When transitions overlap, addition double-counts and the DP is wrong.

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) space

`dp[i]` depends only on the previous two values, so two variables suffice.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;

    long long prev = 1;        // dp[0]
    long long cur = 1;         // dp[1]

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
        // Compute before overwriting — a classic ordering bug otherwise.
        long long next = prev + cur;
        prev = cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    cout << cur << '\n';
}
```

For `n = 1` the loop never runs and `cur` is already 1. Correct without a special case.

## The naive version, and why it fails

```cpp
// O(2^n). At n = 45 this is ~3.5 * 10^13 calls.
long long ways(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2);
}
```

The same subproblem is recomputed exponentially often. Adding a memo array turns it into O(n) with no change to the recurrence — the progression the DP lesson walks through.

## Why `long long`

`fib(46) = 1836311903`, which fits in a signed 32-bit int with very little room. Using `long long` costs nothing and removes the question. The constraint `n <= 45` exists precisely so the answer stays bounded.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Naive recursion | O(2^n) | O(n) stack |
| Memoised | O(n) | O(n) |
| Tabulated | O(n) | O(n) |
| Rolled | O(n) | O(1) |

