editorial:
## Why greedy fails

Taking the largest house first is wrong: on `2 7 9 3 1`, greedy takes 9, then must skip 7 and 3, leaving 2 and 1 for a total of 12 — which happens to be right. But on `2 1 1 2` greedy takes a 2, then the other 2, total 4; correct. Try `4 1 1 4 1 1 4`: greedy works again. The failure is subtler — `2 7 9 3 1` with values changed to `5 1 1 5 10`: greedy takes 10, skips 5, takes 5 and 1 → wait, it takes 10 then can take index 0,1,2 → 5+1 = 6, total 16; optimal is 5+5+10 = 20. Greedy by largest value has no exchange argument, so use DP.

## The four questions

- **State:** `dp[i]` = the best total considering the first `i` houses.
- **Transition:** either skip house `i` (`dp[i-1]`) or take it, which forces skipping `i-1` (`dp[i-2] + a[i]`).
- **Base:** `dp[0] = 0`, `dp[1] = a[0]`.
- **Order:** increasing `i`.

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) space

Since `dp[i]` depends only on the previous two, carry two variables.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    // best_excluding: best total not using the previous house
    // best_including: best total that may use the previous house
    long long best_excluding = 0;
    long long best_including = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long value;
        cin >> value;
        // Compute BOTH from the old values before assigning either. Writing
        // best_including first would feed the new value into the second line.
        long long take = best_excluding + value;
        long long skip = max(best_excluding, best_including);
        best_including = take;
        best_excluding = skip;
    }
    cout << max(best_excluding, best_including) << '\n';
}
```

## Dry run on `2 7 9 3 1`

| value | take = excl + v | skip = max(excl, incl) | excl | incl |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| start | | | 0 | 0 |
| 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 |
| 7 | 7 | 2 | 2 | 7 |
| 9 | 11 | 7 | 7 | 11 |
| 3 | 10 | 11 | 11 | 10 |
| 1 | 12 | 11 | 11 | 12 |

Answer `max(11, 12) = 12`.

## Edge cases

- `n = 0` prints `0`; the loop never runs.
- `n = 1` prints `a[0]`, since `best_including` becomes `a[0]`.
- All values 0 prints `0`.

Because all values are non-negative here, starting both accumulators at 0 is correct. If negative values were allowed, "take nothing" versus "must take something" would need deciding, exactly as in the maximum-subarray problem.

## Complexity

O(n) time, O(1) space. The tabulated version is O(n) space and equally correct — roll it only after the table version works.

