## The O(n^2) DP — write this first

- **State:** `dp[i]` = length of the longest increasing subsequence *ending at* `i`.
- **Transition:** `dp[i] = 1 + max(dp[j])` over all `j < i` with `a[j] < a[i]`.
- **Base:** every `dp[i]` starts at 1 — the element alone.

```cpp
vector<int> dp(n, 1);
int best = n ? 1 : 0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
    for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j)
        if (a[j] < a[i]) dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
    best = max(best, dp[i]);
}
```

Correct, clear, and 10^10 operations at `n = 100000`. Too slow — but it is the reference the fast version must agree with.

## The O(n log n) solution

Maintain an array `tails` where `tails[k]` is the **smallest possible tail value** of an increasing subsequence of length `k+1` found so far.

Two facts make this work:

1. `tails` is always sorted ascending, so it can be binary searched.
2. Keeping tails as small as possible is never worse — a smaller tail accepts at least as many future elements.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    vector<long long> tails;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;

        // lower_bound: the first tail >= x. For STRICTLY increasing, this
        // replaces an equal tail rather than extending past it, so duplicates
        // never lengthen the answer. (For non-decreasing, use upper_bound —
        // one word, different problem.)
        auto it = lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);

        if (it == tails.end()) tails.push_back(x);   // x extends the longest
        else *it = x;                                // x improves this length
    }
    cout << tails.size() << '\n';
}
```

## What `tails` is and is not

`tails` is **not** an actual increasing subsequence — its contents can be a mixture from different subsequences. Only its *length* is meaningful. People often try to print `tails` as the answer subsequence and get a wrong one; recovering the actual subsequence needs a parent-pointer array alongside.

## Dry run on `10 9 2 5 3 7 101 18`

| x | action | tails |
|---|---|---|
| 10 | append | `[10]` |
| 9 | replace 10 | `[9]` |
| 2 | replace 9 | `[2]` |
| 5 | append | `[2,5]` |
| 3 | replace 5 | `[2,3]` |
| 7 | append | `[2,3,7]` |
| 101 | append | `[2,3,7,101]` |
| 18 | replace 101 | `[2,3,7,18]` |

Length 4. Note the final `tails` is `2 3 7 18`, which *is* a valid LIS here — but that is a coincidence, not a guarantee.

## `lower_bound` versus `upper_bound`

This is the detail that decides correctness:

- **Strictly increasing** → `lower_bound`. An equal element replaces, so `7 7 7` gives 1.
- **Non-decreasing** → `upper_bound`. An equal element extends, so `7 7 7` gives 3.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| DP | O(n^2) | O(n) |
| Patience / binary search | O(n log n) | O(n) |

