editorial:
## The canonical 2-D DP

- **State:** `dp[i][j]` = LCS length of the first `i` characters of `a` and the first `j` of `b`.
- **Transition:**
  - If `a[i-1] == b[j-1]`, those characters can be paired: `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1`.
  - Otherwise one of them must be dropped: `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])`.
- **Base:** row 0 and column 0 are all 0 — an empty string shares nothing.
- **Order:** increasing `i`, then increasing `j`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    string a, b;
    cin >> a >> b;
    int n = (int)a.size(), m = (int)b.size();

    // (n+1) x (m+1), so index 0 means "empty prefix" and the first character
    // needs no special case — the same +1 trick as a prefix-sum array.
    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
            // a[i-1] with dp[i][j]: the table is 1-indexed, the string is 0-
            // indexed. Write it deliberately once and stay consistent.
            if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            else dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        }
    }
    cout << dp[n][m] << '\n';
}
```

## Why the equal case does not also need the max

When the characters match, pairing them is never worse than dropping either one. That is a small proof worth knowing: any common subsequence of the two prefixes can be rearranged to end with this matched pair without getting shorter. So `dp[i-1][j-1] + 1` dominates and no `max` is needed.

## Rolling to O(m) space

Row `i` depends only on row `i-1`, so two rows suffice:

```cpp
vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j)
        cur[j] = (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) ? prev[j - 1] + 1
                                        : max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);
    swap(prev, cur);        // O(1): swaps the buffers, not the contents
}
cout << prev[m] << '\n';
```

At 1000 × 1000 the full table is 4 MB and fits comfortably — so roll it only after the table version is verified correct. Rolling introduces read-after-write bugs that are much harder to find than a memory limit.

## Related problems, same table

- **Edit distance** — same shape, three transitions instead of two (insert, delete, replace).
- **Longest common substring** (contiguous) — reset to 0 on a mismatch instead of taking a max, and track the best cell rather than the corner.
- **Longest palindromic subsequence** — the LCS of the string with its own reverse.

## Complexity

O(n · m) time. O(n · m) space, or O(min(n, m)) rolled.

