editorial:
## Why greedy fails

Taking the longest matching prefix at each step is wrong. On `s = "catsandog"` with dictionary `{cats, dog, sand, and, cat}`, greedy takes `cats`, then `and`, then is stuck on `og` — and concludes false. It *is* false here, but on `s = "aaaaab"` with `{aaaa, aaa, b}`, greedy takes `aaaa` then cannot split `ab`, while `aaa` + ... also fails — a case where the wrong first choice loses a valid split exists for `{aa, aaa}` on `"aaaaa"`: taking `aa, aa` leaves `a`; taking `aa, aaa` works. One wrong choice dooms the rest, so you need to consider all of them.

## The four questions

- **State:** `dp[i]` = can the first `i` characters be split entirely into dictionary words?
- **Transition:** `dp[i]` is true if there is some `j < i` where `dp[j]` is true **and** `s[j..i-1]` is a dictionary word.
- **Base:** `dp[0] = true` — the empty prefix is trivially splittable. This is the base case that makes everything work.
- **Order:** increasing `i`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    string s;
    cin >> s;
    int n;
    cin >> n;

    unordered_set<string> dict;
    size_t longest = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        string w;
        cin >> w;
        longest = max(longest, w.size());
        dict.insert(w);
    }

    int len = (int)s.size();
    vector<char> dp(len + 1, false);
    dp[0] = true;                       // the empty prefix splits

    for (int i = 1; i <= len; ++i) {
        // Only look back as far as the longest dictionary word. Without this
        // the inner loop is O(len) instead of O(20) — correct either way,
        // but needlessly slower.
        for (int j = i - 1; j >= 0 && (size_t)(i - j) <= longest; --j) {
            if (!dp[j]) continue;       // nothing splittable ends at j
            if (dict.count(s.substr(j, i - j))) {
                dp[i] = true;
                break;                  // one witness is enough
            }
        }
    }
    cout << (dp[len] ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

## Why `dp[0] = true` is essential

It encodes "there is a valid split ending just before the first character", which is what lets the first word be matched at all. Set it to `false` and the entire table stays false — the single most common bug on this problem, and it produces a plausible `false` rather than a crash.

## Dry run on `leetcode` with `{leet, code}`

`dp[0] = true`. Scanning forward, `dp[4]` becomes true because `dp[0]` is true and `s[0..3] = "leet"` is in the dictionary. Then `dp[8]` becomes true because `dp[4]` is true and `s[4..7] = "code"` is in the dictionary. Answer: `dp[8] = true`.

## Note on `vector<char>` rather than `vector<bool>`

`vector<bool>` is a bit-packed specialisation — it is not a normal container, cannot hand out a `bool&`, and is slower per access. For a DP flag array, `vector<char>` is the better default.

## Complexity

O(len · L) hash lookups where `L` is the longest word, each costing O(L) to build the substring — so O(len · L²) overall, or about 300 · 400 = 120000 operations here. A trie over the dictionary removes the `substr` allocation entirely and is the better structure at scale.

