editorial:
## The four questions

- **State:** `dp[r][c]` = number of paths from the start to cell `(r, c)`.
- **Transition:** you can only arrive from above or from the left, and those path sets are disjoint, so `dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1]`.
- **Base:** the entire first row and first column are 1 — there is exactly one way to walk straight along an edge.
- **Order:** row by row, left to right.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int m, n;
    cin >> m >> n;

    // Initialising everything to 1 handles both base cases at once: the first
    // row and first column are already correct and never written again.
    vector<vector<long long>> dp(m, vector<long long>(n, 1));

    for (int r = 1; r < m; ++r)
        for (int c = 1; c < n; ++c)
            dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1];

    cout << dp[m - 1][n - 1] << '\n';
}
```

## Rolled to O(n) space

Each row depends only on the row above, and within a row only on the cell to the left — so a single array updated in place works:

```cpp
vector<long long> row(n, 1);
for (int r = 1; r < m; ++r)
    for (int c = 1; c < n; ++c)
        row[c] += row[c - 1];     // row[c] is still the row above; row[c-1]
                                  // is already this row. Both correct.
cout << row[n - 1] << '\n';
```

That in-place update is subtle and worth staring at: when we read `row[c]` it has not yet been updated this pass, so it holds `dp[r-1][c]`; `row[c-1]` *has* been updated, so it holds `dp[r][c-1]`. Exactly the two values needed. Left-to-right order is load-bearing — reverse it and the answer is wrong.

## The closed form

Every path is a sequence of exactly `m-1` downs and `n-1` rights, in some order. So the count is the number of ways to choose which of the `m+n-2` moves are downs:

```
C(m + n - 2, m - 1)
```

For `m = 3, n = 7`: `C(8, 2) = 28`. Computing it needs care to avoid overflow in the intermediate factorials — multiply and divide alternately rather than computing `(m+n-2)!` outright.

The DP is O(m·n) = 10000 operations here, so the combinatorial shortcut buys nothing. It is worth knowing because it generalises badly — add a single obstacle to the grid and the formula collapses while the DP needs one extra line.

## Edge cases

`m = 1` or `n = 1` gives exactly 1 path. The all-ones initialisation handles it: the loops never execute.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Table | O(m·n) | O(m·n) |
| Rolled | O(m·n) | O(n) |
| Combinatorics | O(min(m,n)) | O(1) |

