editorial:
## The algorithm

Scan every cell. When you find unvisited land, increment the count and **flood fill** the whole connected region so it is never counted again. The count of flood fills is the number of islands.

The grid is an implicit graph: cells are vertices, and the four orthogonal steps are the edges. No adjacency list is needed.

## Iterative flood fill — O(rows · cols)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int rows, cols;
    cin >> rows >> cols;
    vector<string> grid(rows);
    for (string& row : grid) cin >> row;

    const int dr[] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
    const int dc[] = {0, 0, -1, 1};
    int islands = 0;

    for (int r = 0; r < rows; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; ++c) {
            if (grid[r][c] != '1') continue;
            ++islands;

            // 🛑 Iterative, not recursive. A 300x300 all-land grid is 90000
            // cells deep; recursive DFS overflows the stack.
            stack<pair<int, int>> st;
            st.push({r, c});
            grid[r][c] = '0';          // mark at PUSH time

            while (!st.empty()) {
                auto [cr, cc] = st.top();
                st.pop();
                for (int d = 0; d < 4; ++d) {
                    int nr = cr + dr[d], nc = cc + dc[d];
                    // Bounds BEFORE access. && short-circuits, so the order
                    // is guaranteed — reversed, this reads out of range.
                    if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;
                    if (grid[nr][nc] != '1') continue;
                    grid[nr][nc] = '0';    // mark before pushing
                    st.push({nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }
    }
    cout << islands << '\n';
}
```

## Mark at push time, not pop time

If you mark only when popping, a cell reachable from two neighbours gets pushed twice. It is still *correct* — the `!= '1'` check catches it on the second pop — but the stack can grow to four times the region size, and on larger problems that is the difference between passing and a memory limit. Marking at push keeps each cell in the stack at most once.

## Mutating the input as the visited marker

Overwriting land with water uses O(1) extra space instead of a separate `visited` array. Legitimate here because the grid is not needed afterwards. If it were, use a `vector<vector<char>> seen` — destroying your input to save memory is a trade, not a free win.

## The DSU alternative

Union every pair of adjacent land cells, then count distinct roots. O(rows · cols · α) — asymptotically the same, more code, and it shines only when the problem adds cells dynamically ("number of islands II"). Flood fill is the right answer here.

## Pitfalls

- **Recursive DFS on a large grid.** Stack overflow.
- **Checking bounds after indexing.**
- **Counting diagonals.** Four directions, not eight — the statement is explicit, and an eight-direction array silently merges islands.
- **Forgetting to mark the start cell** before the loop, which would push it again from its own neighbour.

## Complexity

O(rows · cols) time — every cell is visited a constant number of times. O(rows · cols) worst-case stack space, for an all-land grid.

