## Restating it as a graph question

Draw an edge from each prerequisite to the course that needs it. All courses are completable exactly when this directed graph has **no cycle** — a cycle means a set of courses that each require another in the set, so none can be first.

So the question is: is this a DAG?

## Kahn's algorithm — O(n + m)

Repeatedly take a course with no outstanding prerequisites. If you can take all `n` this way, there was no cycle.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<vector<int>> adj(n);
    vector<int> in_degree(n, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int a, b;
        cin >> a >> b;
        adj[b].push_back(a);     // b -> a: b unlocks a
        ++in_degree[a];
    }

    queue<int> ready;
    for (int v = 0; v < n; ++v)
        if (in_degree[v] == 0) ready.push(v);   // nothing blocks these

    int taken = 0;
    while (!ready.empty()) {
        int u = ready.front();
        ready.pop();
        ++taken;
        for (int v : adj[u])
            if (--in_degree[v] == 0) ready.push(v);   // last blocker gone
    }

    // Anything left never reached in-degree 0, so it sits in a cycle.
    cout << (taken == n ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```

## Why `taken == n` detects a cycle

Every vertex in a cycle has at least one incoming edge from another cycle member, which is itself never removed. So no cycle member ever reaches in-degree 0, and the count falls short. The shortfall *is* the cycle detection — no separate pass needed.

## The DFS alternative — three states

```cpp
// 0 = unvisited, 1 = on the current path, 2 = fully explored
vector<int> state(n, 0);
function<bool(int)> has_cycle = [&](int u) {
    state[u] = 1;
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (state[v] == 1) return true;            // back edge -> cycle
        if (state[v] == 0 && has_cycle(v)) return true;
        // state[v] == 2 is fine: already explored and acyclic.
    }
    state[u] = 2;
    return false;
};
```

**Two states are not enough.** With a plain `visited` flag, a vertex legitimately reachable by two different paths — a diamond `0->1->3`, `0->2->3` — is reported as a cycle. The third state distinguishes "on the current path" from "finished and clean", and that distinction is the entire algorithm.

Note this version is recursive; at `n = 2000` the depth is fine, but on a 10^5-vertex chain it would overflow. Kahn's has no such limit, which is one reason to prefer it.

## Direction of the edges

The pair `a b` means "b before a", so the edge runs `b -> a`. Reversing it gives a graph whose cycles are the same — so this problem still answers correctly — but the *topological order* you would print is reversed. The sibling problem "course schedule II" asks for the order, and there the direction matters.

## Duplicate pairs

The constraints permit them. Kahn's handles them naturally: a duplicate raises the in-degree twice and is decremented twice. No deduplication needed.

## Complexity

O(n + m) time and space, for both approaches.

