## Sort by start, then one pass

Sorting is what makes this linear afterwards: once intervals are in ascending order of start, **any overlap must be with the most recently merged interval.** Nothing earlier can reach forward past it. That single fact turns an all-pairs comparison into one sweep.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<pair<long long, long long>> intervals(n);
    for (auto& [l, r] : intervals) cin >> l >> r;

    // Sorting pairs compares .first then .second — exactly "by start, then
    // by end", which is what we want.
    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());

    vector<pair<long long, long long>> merged;
    for (auto [start, end] : intervals) {
        // `<=` merges touching intervals too: [1,4] and [4,5] become [1,5].
        // Use `<` if the problem treats touching as separate — read it.
        if (!merged.empty() && start <= merged.back().second) {
            // 🛑 max(), not assignment. [1,10] followed by [2,3] must stay
            // [1,10]; writing `= end` would shrink it to [1,3].
            merged.back().second = max(merged.back().second, end);
        } else {
            merged.push_back({start, end});
        }
    }

    for (auto [l, r] : merged) cout << l << ' ' << r << '\n';
}
```

## The two details that decide correctness

**`max` when extending.** A fully-contained interval must not shrink the one containing it. Test it with `1 10` then `2 3` — the expected output is a single `1 10`.

**`<=` versus `<`.** This problem merges touching intervals, so `<=`. The same template with `<` solves the variant where `[1,4]` and `[4,5]` stay separate. Nothing in the code tells you which; only the statement does.

## Why not merge in place

Tempting, but an in-place merge needs to shift elements, which is O(n) per merge. Building a separate output vector is O(n) total and much clearer. The output is at most as long as the input, so the memory is not a concern.

## The family of interval problems

All of them are "sort, then sweep"; only the sort key and the sweep differ:

| Problem | Sort by | Sweep |
|---|---|---|
| Merge intervals | start | Extend the last merged |
| Insert interval | start | Three phases: before, merge, after |
| Non-overlapping intervals (max keep) | **end** | Greedy take if `start >= last_end` |
| Meeting rooms II (max simultaneous) | — | Sweep line with ±1 events |

Sorting by start when the greedy needs end is the classic wrong answer — see the intervals lesson for why earliest-finish is the safe greedy.

## Complexity

O(n log n) time, dominated entirely by the sort. O(n) space for the output.

