## Restating it

The minimum number of rooms equals the **maximum number of meetings happening simultaneously**. Once you see that, the problem stops being about scheduling and becomes about counting overlaps.

## Approach A: sweep line — O(n log n)

Turn each meeting into two events: `+1` at its start, `-1` at its end. Sort all events by time and track the running total; its maximum is the answer.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    vector<pair<long long, int>> events;
    events.reserve(n * 2);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long s, e;
        cin >> s >> e;
        events.push_back({s, +1});
        events.push_back({e, -1});
    }

    // 🛑 The tie-break is the whole correctness argument. Sorting pairs
    // compares .second when times are equal, and -1 < +1 — so an END is
    // processed before a START at the same instant. That is exactly what
    // makes a meeting ending at 10 free the room for one starting at 10.
    sort(events.begin(), events.end());

    int current = 0, best = 0;
    for (auto [time, delta] : events) {
        current += delta;
        best = max(best, current);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

The tie-break falls out of `pair`'s lexicographic comparison rather than needing a custom comparator — a small piece of luck worth noticing, and worth making explicit with a comment because it is otherwise invisible.

## Approach B: a min-heap of end times — O(n log n)

Sort meetings by start. Keep a min-heap of the end times of rooms currently in use. For each meeting, free every room that has finished, then allocate one.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<pair<long long, long long>> meetings(n);
    for (auto& [s, e] : meetings) cin >> s >> e;

    sort(meetings.begin(), meetings.end());        // by start time

    // Min-heap: the root is the room that frees up soonest, which is the only
    // one worth checking. The greater<> incantation makes it a min-heap.
    priority_queue<long long, vector<long long>, greater<long long>> busy;

    size_t best = 0;
    for (auto [start, end] : meetings) {
        // `<=` because a meeting ending exactly at `start` frees its room.
        while (!busy.empty() && busy.top() <= start) busy.pop();
        busy.push(end);
        best = max(best, busy.size());
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

This version answers a slightly richer question — it also tells you *which* room each meeting goes in — at the cost of being a little longer.

## Why a min-heap and not a max-heap

The only room that could possibly be free is the one finishing earliest. If that one is still busy, every other is too. A max-heap would hand you the room finishing *last*, which tells you nothing useful.

## Pitfalls

- **`<` instead of `<=`** at the boundary. On `7 10` and `10 12` the answer is 1; with `<` you get 2. The statement is explicit about touching meetings — read it, because both conventions appear in different problems.
- **Sorting by end time** and trying the earliest-finish greedy. That answers a different question (the maximum number of non-overlapping meetings), not the room count.
- **A max-heap.**
- **Scanning all pairs** for overlap: O(n²), too slow at 10^5.

## Complexity

Both approaches are O(n log n) time and O(n) space, dominated by the sort.

