```

One call, linear average time, and it does less work than a full sort because it only recurses into the half containing the target.

## Approach C: a min-heap of size k — O(n log k)

The one to reach for when `k` is small or the data arrives as a stream you cannot store.

```cpp
priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> keep;   // MIN-heap
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    int x;
    cin >> x;
    keep.push(x);
    if ((int)keep.size() > k) keep.pop();    // evict the weakest
}
cout << keep.top() << '\n';                  // the k-th largest
```

The counter-intuitive part, worth stating plainly: to keep the `k` **largest** elements you use a **min**-heap. Its root is the smallest of the current best `k`, which is exactly the element to discard when a better one arrives. At the end, that root is the `k`-th largest.

Memory is O(k) rather than O(n) — so this works on 10^9 streamed values with `k = 10`, which neither sorting nor `nth_element` can do.

## Approach D: quickselect by hand

Partition like quicksort, but recurse only into the side containing position `k-1`. O(n) average, O(n²) worst case on adversarial input — which is why `nth_element` (typically introselect, with a fallback to guarantee O(n)) is the better choice. Worth implementing once for understanding; not worth writing under time pressure.

## Which to use

| | Time | Space | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sort | O(n log n) | O(1) | Default. Shortest correct answer |
| `nth_element` | O(n) average | O(1) | One line, strictly better than sorting |
| Min-heap of k | O(n log k) | **O(k)** | `k` much smaller than `n`, or streaming |
| Quickselect | O(n) avg, O(n²) worst | O(1) | Learning exercise |

## Pitfalls

- **Deduplicating first.** Different question.
- **`a[k]` instead of `a[k-1]`.** `k` is 1-based, indices are 0-based.
- **A max-heap for the k largest.**
- **`top()` after the final `pop()`.** Check the order of operations.

