## Approach A: Gauss's sum — O(n) time, O(1) space

The full range `0..n` sums to `n(n+1)/2`. Subtract what is actually present and the difference is the missing value.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    long long n;
    cin >> n;

    // 🛑 64-bit arithmetic. At n = 100000 the expected sum is about 5*10^9,
    // which overflows a 32-bit int. `n * (n + 1) / 2` in int is the classic
    // wrong answer here — see the constraints lesson on 1LL.
    long long expected = n * (n + 1) / 2;
    long long actual = 0;
    for (long long i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        actual += x;
    }
    cout << expected - actual << '\n';
}
```

The `/ 2` is exact without floating point because `n(n+1)` is always even — one of two consecutive integers is.

## Approach B: XOR — no overflow at all

XOR every index `0..n` together with every value present. Each number that *is* present appears twice and cancels; the missing one appears once, from the index side only.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    // Seed with n: the loop below covers indices 0..n-1, so n itself must be
    // folded in separately to represent the full 0..n range.
    int result = n;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        result ^= i ^ x;
    }
    cout << result << '\n';
}
```

This is the better answer. It cannot overflow — XOR never produces a value outside the bit width — so the whole category of bug that Approach A has to guard against simply does not arise.

## Why `result` starts at `n`

The range is `0..n`, which has `n+1` values, but the loop only runs `n` times and so only generates indices `0..n-1`. Seeding with `n` supplies the last one. Starting at 0 instead gives a wrong answer whenever the missing number is not `n` — and happens to be right when it is, which makes it an easy bug to miss.

## Dry run on `3 0 1` with n = 3

| i | x | i ^ x | result |
|---|---|---|---|
| start | | | 3 |
| 0 | 3 | 3 | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 2 | 1 | 3 | 2 |

Answer 2.

## The alternatives

| Approach | Time | Space | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sort and scan | O(n log n) | O(1) | Slower; mutates input |
| Hash set | O(n) | **O(n)** | Breaks the space requirement |
| Gauss sum | O(n) | O(1) | Needs 64-bit care |
| **XOR** | **O(n)** | **O(1)** | No overflow possible |

## Pitfalls

- **32-bit overflow** in the sum version.
- **Seeding the XOR with 0** instead of `n`.
- **Assuming sorted input.** Neither approach needs it.
- **Edge case `n = 1`:** the input is either `0` (missing 1) or `1` (missing 0). Both fall out of the formulas with no special case — check them.

