for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i)
    if ((value >> i) & 1) ++count;
```

Always 32 iterations regardless of the input.

## Approach B: clear the lowest set bit — O(number of set bits)

The trick worth memorising: **`v & (v - 1)` clears the lowest set bit.**

Why: subtracting 1 flips the lowest set bit to 0 and turns every bit below it to 1. ANDing with the original keeps only the bits above, so exactly one 1 bit disappears per iteration.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    unsigned long long value;
    cin >> value;

    int count = 0;
    while (value) {
        value &= value - 1;        // clears exactly one set bit
        ++count;
    }
    cout << count << '\n';
}
```

For `11 = 1011`: `1011 -> 1010 -> 1000 -> 0000`. Three iterations, three set bits. On a sparse number like `2^31` this is one iteration instead of 32.

## Approach C: the built-in

```cpp
cout << __builtin_popcountll(value) << '\n';
```

Compiles to a single `popcnt` instruction on hardware that has one. Use `__builtin_popcount` for `unsigned int` and `__builtin_popcountll` for `unsigned long long` — passing a 64-bit value to the 32-bit version silently truncates and gives a wrong answer for large inputs.

C++20 also offers `std::popcount` from `<bit>`, which is the portable spelling.

## Why unsigned

Two reasons, both real:

1. The constraint allows values up to `4294967295`, which exceeds `INT_MAX`. Reading into a signed `int` fails.
2. Right-shifting a negative signed value is implementation-defined — it may sign-extend, so `>> i` never empties and a `while (value)` loop never terminates. Bit manipulation should use unsigned types by default.

## The related tricks

| Expression | Effect |
|---|---|
| `v & -v` | Isolate the lowest set bit |
| `v & (v - 1)` | Clear the lowest set bit |
| `v` bitwise-or `(v + 1)` | Set the lowest clear bit |
| `v && !(v & (v - 1))` | Is `v` a power of two? |
| `__builtin_ctz(v)` | Index of the lowest set bit — **undefined for 0** |

The power-of-two test follows directly: a power of two has exactly one set bit, so clearing it leaves zero. The `v &&` guards against 0, which has no set bits and is not a power of two.

## Edge cases

- `0` must print `0`. The `while` loop never runs.
- `4294967295` is all 32 bits set, so the answer is `32` — and this is the case that catches a signed `int` read.

## Complexity

O(number of set bits) for Approach B, which is at most 32. O(1) space.
